-- title:
--   Theorem 17 — $\mathbb P[Z_X\subseteq K]\ge\mathrm{Vol}_n(K\ominus Z_{\mathbb E[X]})/\mathrm{Vol}_n(K)$ for independent $X\ge0$
-- statement:
--   Let $K\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ be a symmetric convex body ($K$ compact, convex, with nonempty interior, and $K=-K$), and let $Z=\sum_{j=1}^m[-a_j,a_j]$ be the zonotope generated by the rows $a_1,\dots,a_m$ of a matrix $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$. For $t\in\mathbb R^m_{\ge0}$ write $Z_t=\{\sum_j y_ja_j:\ |y_j|\le t_j\}$. Let $X=(X_1,\dots,X_m)$ be a random vector with values in $\mathbb R^m_{\ge0}$ whose coordinates are independent integrable random variables, and let $\mathbb E[X]=(\mathbb E[X_1],\dots,\mathbb E[X_m])$. Then
--   $$\mathbb P\big[Z_X\subseteq K\big]\ \ge\ \frac{\mathrm{Vol}_n\big(K\ominus Z_{\mathbb E[X]}\big)}{\mathrm{Vol}_n(K)},$$
--   where $K\ominus L=\{x:\ x+L\subseteq K\}$ is Minkowski subtraction.
--
--   With $K=(1+\varepsilon)Z$ and $X$ uniform on $\mathbf 1+[-1,1]^m$ it gives $\mathbb P[Z_{\mathbf 1+x}\subseteq(1+\varepsilon)Z]\ge(\varepsilon/(1+\varepsilon))^n$; Theorem 20 sharpens this to a bound $c^n$ for $Z_{\mathbf 1+x}\subseteq Z$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $X$ is a family of measurable functions on a probability space $(\Omega,\mu)$, mutually independent (`iIndepFun`), pointwise nonnegative and integrable. Both volumes are of bounded sets and are taken as real numbers; $\mathrm{Vol}_n(K)>0$ for a convex body.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: X ∈ ℝ^m_{≥0} a random vector with independent coordinates, and E[X] → X : Fin m → Ω → ℝ on a probability space, each X i measurable and integrable, iIndepFun X μ, X i ω ≥ 0 for all i, ω; reason: a random variable is measurable, and integrability only excludes the cases where E[X] ∈ ℝ^m, which the statement uses, is undefined
--   - encoding: symmetric convex body K; Z generated by m segments → IsConvexBody K and ∀ x ∈ K, −x ∈ K; Z = zonotope A 1 with Z_t = zonotope A t for the rows of A ∈ ℝ^{m×n}; reason: the same objects; every list of m vectors is the row list of a matrix
--   - encoding: Lebesgue volume Vol_n (a value in [0,∞]) and probability P → (volume ·).toReal and (μ ·).toReal; reason: K and K ⊖ Z_{E[X]} are bounded, so their volumes are finite, and probabilities are at most 1
-- source:
--   Victor Reis, Thomas Rothvoss, Linear-size ℓ1 sparsifiers, STOC 2027 submission #162 (October 2026; PDF SHA-256 5137e7dee3cd303bd508f90f5f42c955632af5dc2ec978d9ef575bae4ce23710), §3.2, Theorem 17, p.9

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_L1Sparsifiers_Main_Zonotope
import Definitions.Def_L1Sparsifiers_Main_ConvexBodyVolumes

namespace L1Sparsifiers.Main.Supplement

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

/-- **Theorem 17** (Reis–Rothvoss, STOC 2027 submission #162, §3.2, p.9).
"Let K ⊆ ℝⁿ be a symmetric convex body and Z ⊆ ℝⁿ be a zonotope generated by m segments. Let
X ∈ ℝ^m_{≥0} be a random vector with independent coordinates. Then
P[Z_X ⊆ K] ≥ Volₙ(K ⊖ Z_{E[X]}) / Volₙ(K)."

`K` is a convex body (compact, convex, nonempty interior) with `K = −K`. `Z` is generated by the
rows of `A ∈ ℝ^{m×n}` and `Z_t = zonotope A t`. `X = (X_1, …, X_m)` lives on a probability space
`(Ω, μ)`; its coordinates are random variables (measurable), mutually independent, nonnegative and
integrable (so that `E[X] = (E[X_1], …, E[X_m])` exists). `⊖` is Minkowski subtraction. Volumes of
the bounded sets `K` and `K ⊖ Z_{E[X]}` are taken as real numbers. -/
theorem theorem_17 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    {m n : ℕ} (K : Set (Fin n → ℝ)) (hK : IsConvexBody K) (hKsymm : ∀ x ∈ K, -x ∈ K)
    (A : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ) (X : Fin m → Ω → ℝ) (hXmeas : ∀ i, Measurable (X i))
    (hXind : iIndepFun X μ) (hXnn : ∀ i ω, 0 ≤ X i ω) (hXint : ∀ i, Integrable (X i) μ) :
    (volume (minkowskiSub K (zonotope A (fun i => ∫ ω, X i ω ∂μ)))).toReal / (volume K).toReal ≤
      (μ {ω | zonotope A (fun i => X i ω) ⊆ K}).toReal := by sorry

end L1Sparsifiers.Main.Supplement
